--- David spares Saul
kicker: Example
sub: “Touch Not the Lord’s Anointed”
big: 1 Samuel 24:6
p: David refused to kill King Saul even when he had the opportunity, because Saul was God’s anointed king. He would not lay a hand on him, choosing instead to honor the position God had given, even while Saul pursued him unjustly.
callout: This illustrates respectful submission to authority while trusting God to deal with the leader.

--- Civil disobedience: when it is wrong
lead: The Bible warns against rebellious or self-willed resistance to authority. Examples of bad civil disobedience include:
panel-warn: Resisting lawful authority | Out of pride or rebellion. [[Romans 13:2]]
panel-warn: Korah’s rebellion | Against Moses and Aaron. God judged the rebels severely. [[Numbers 16:1-35|Numbers 16]]
panel-warn: A king like the nations | The people’s demand for a king like the nations, rejecting God’s direct rule, led to later troubles. [[1 Samuel 8:4-9|1 Samuel 8]]
callout: **General principle:** Civil disobedience is never justified when it stems from personal rebellion, selfishness, or refusal to submit to God-ordained order. We are called to submit for the Lord’s sake and for conscience, unless a clear command of God is violated.

